 <section class="section alt"><div class="shell"><div class="section-head"><span class="kicker">The fast screen</span><h2>Four ways a purchase goes wrong</h2><p class="lede">Fit is not just round versus elongated. A product can match the bowl and still fail at the tank, bolts, valve, or outlet.</p></div><div class="grid"><article class="card"><span class="number">01</span><h3>Rear geometry</h3><p>French curves and rising porcelain can collide with the housing of a seat or attachment.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="number">02</span><h3>Mounting dimensions</h3><p>Bolt spacing, bowl length, and the flat area behind the holes must match the exact installation drawing.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="number">03</span><h3>Plumbing access</h3><p>Skirted toilets can hide the tank connection where a standard T-adapter expects to go.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="number">04</span><h3>Power</h3><p>Electric seats need a suitable receptacle and product-specific cord reach—not a permanent extension-cord workaround.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="number">05</span><h3>Model variation</h3><p>Compact elongated and remolded toilet versions can defeat broad model-name claims.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="number">06</span><h3>Return limitations</h3><p>Hygiene products can have strict unused-only policies, making pre-purchase verification unusually valuable.</p></article></div></div></section>
